One undrainable column is one fault, and the store reports it once.

The declaration draws two refusals. KORU160 explains the fault and hints the fix:
every exit from the state returns a value, so bolt on `! discharge`. KORU161 then
states the same refusal again in a sentence that calls a count of ZERO
"ambiguous" — and an author who reads both is told the store cannot pick from
nothing.

store.kz states the rule this breaks in its own words, four lines above the site:
the difference between "your library is missing something" and "pick one" is the
whole difference, so the sentence has to say which. KORU160 says which. KORU161
un-says it.

PIN: one diagnostic for one fault, and the surviving one is the one that
distinguishes the two reasons and carries the hint.

690_058 is the count>1 face of this program and stays green either way, because
it asserts only what the output CONTAINS and so cannot see a duplicate — which is
why the pair went unpinned. 11747c79 stopped a refused declaration from
cascading into ghost errors about a store the author can see; this is the same
one-mistake-one-error shape one layer in, where both errors are about the mistake
itself.

